  data:  # 4b05
    kind: invariant  # 4b05
    title: "Data returned by a subprocess mints no taint source in any language: subprocess is a sink-only boundary, so cmd.Output()/check_output() crossings are unrepresented"  # 4b05
    status: violated  # 4b05
    priority: 1  # 4b05
    parent:  # 4b05
    tags:  # 4b05
    - taint  # 4b05
    - io_boundary  # 4b05
    - catalogue  # 4b05
    - analysis_quality  # 4b05
    isbefore: []  # 4b05
    duplicate_of: []  # 4b05
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4b05
    pr_ref:  # 4b05
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-31 while shipping WI-lipis's first deliverable. Not found by\nlooking for it: removing a WRONG row exposed what it had been standing in for,\nwhich is the same way INV-zihor was found from the io_lib removal.\n\nTHE GAP. Data RETURNED by a subprocess is chosen by the far side, so under\nADR-0049 ruling 1 (\"does this call return a value whose content the far side\nchose?\") it is a TRANSFER and should mint a taint source. No catalogue in the\ntree treats it as one. Measured across all shipped io_primitives YAMLs, every\nrow that reads a subprocess's output files under `subprocess`, and `subprocess`\nis NOT in AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP:\n\n  c        stdlib :: system, popen                      subprocess, not a source\n  go       os/exec.Cmd :: Run Start Output CombinedOutput  subprocess, not a source\n  python   subprocess :: run Popen call check_call check_output\n                                                        subprocess, not a source\n  python   os :: system popen execl...                  subprocess, not a source\n\nThe only stdin-shaped source anywhere is python's `sys.stdin`. So `subprocess`\nis a SINK-ONLY boundary across the whole catalogue, and the return direction is\nunmodelled in every language.\n\nHOW IT SURFACED, with the measurement that makes it concrete. WI-lipis stops\n`bufio.NewScanner(strings.NewReader(s))` from minting an untrusted_input source\n(correctly: the scanner reads memory, not a channel). Paired A/B on the four\ncohort Go repositories that carry such sites, both arms cold:\n\n  beads    1974 -> 1957 findings   untrusted-input-no-subprocess  22 -> 17\n  cilium    623 ->  612 findings   untrusted-input-no-subprocess   3 ->  1\n  caddy     144 ->  144            (no in-memory sites)\n  jaeger    135 ->  135            (no in-memory sites)\n\nALL SEVEN removed evidence rows were then read back against source. EVERY ONE\nwraps a buffer filled from a subprocess's stdout:\n\n  beads  cmd/bd/import.go:633   bytes.NewReader(output)     <- gitShowCmd.Output()\n  beads  cmd/bd/init.go:934     bytes.NewReader(output)     <- cmd.Output()\n  beads  cmd/bd/jira.go:389     strings.NewReader(string(output)) <- python3 cmd.Output()\n  beads  cmd/bd/jira.go:480     strings.NewReader(string(output)) <- cmd.Output()\n  beads  cmd/bd/status.go:160   strings.NewReader(string(output)) <- git log cmd.Output()\n  cilium pkg/datapath/iptables/iptables.go:207   strings.NewReader(rules)\n                                                <- prog.runProgOutput(iptables -S)\n  cilium pkg/datapath/iptables/iptables.go:1023  strings.NewReader(rules)\n                                                <- prog.runProgOutput(iptables -S)\n\nZERO of the seven were a genuinely in-process string.\n\n*** THIS REFUTES WI-lipis'S OWN CHARACTERISATION OF THE CASE. *** That item's\ndiscussion calls the 19.1% strings/bytes.NewReader bucket \"pure false-positive\nremoval\". On this corpus it is not pure: the ROW is wrong (the scanner crosses\nnothing, and it claimed ipc_recv), but the FLOW tracked real external data\narriving one hop earlier. So WI-lipis's first deliverable is a net recall\nREDUCTION until this item is fixed, and that is disclosed rather than counted\nas a precision win.\n\nWHY THE WRONG ROW WAS STILL REMOVED rather than kept as a stand-in. Keeping a\nrow that misattributes WHERE a crossing happens, because it happens to\nco-occur with a real one, makes the correct fix look like a regression and\nmakes any measurement spanning the two changes uninterpretable. That is the\nreasoning the owner already accepted for io_lib -> INV-zihor, and this is the\nsame shape one language over.\n\nBEFORE BUILDING. This is NEW SURFACE in every language at once, not a Go\ninconsistency, so under the owner's band ruling it carries its own precision\nestimate. Two questions to answer first, neither of which is answered here:\n  1. Which boundary? `ipc_recv` is the honest fit (a pipe from another\n     process), but it collapses into `untrusted_input` alongside net_recv and\n     db_read, so a claim cannot then distinguish \"the network told me\" from\n     \"a program I launched told me\". INV-tutar split env_read/host_info_read\n     for exactly that reason and is the precedent to follow or to reject\n     explicitly.\n  2. What is the precision? A subprocess whose ARGUMENTS the program chose\n     (git log, iptables -S) is a very different trust story from one whose\n     arguments came from outside. The seven instances above are all the\n     former. A source that fires on every `cmd.Output()` may be right in\n     principle and mostly vacuous in practice -- which is the INV-nular F6\n     question asked in the other direction, and it should be measured on the\n     cohort before any row is added."  # 4b05
    fields:  # 4b05
      statement: "A call that returns a value whose content a subprocess chose is a transfer under ADR-0049 ruling 1 and must mint a taint source; today no catalogue in any language does so."  # 4b05
      root_cause: "Every io_primitives row that reads a subprocess's output is filed under the 'subprocess' boundary, which is absent from AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, so the return direction of a subprocess is unmodelled catalogue-wide."  # 4b05
