- op: discuss  # c3cc
  at: '2026-08-31T09:50:45Z'  # c3cc
  by: agent  # c3cc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c3cc
  clock: 3  # c3cc
  nonce: c3cc  # c3cc
  message: "THE BOUNDARY QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE TREE ANSWERED IT BEFORE I DID --\nWHICH ALSO MAKES THIS ITEM'S OWN DIAGNOSIS HALF WRONG.\n\nThis item states that `subprocess` is \"a SINK-ONLY boundary in EVERY language\"\nand that \"no catalogue in the tree treats [the return direction] as a source\",\nand asks which boundary to choose, naming INV-tutar as the precedent to follow\nor reject. TWO CATALOGUES ALREADY CHOSE.\n\n  rust.yaml   ipc_recv   std::process::Child.wait_with_output\n                         std::process::ChildStdout.read (+ 4 siblings)\n                         std::process::ChildStderr.read (+ 4 siblings)\n  python.yaml ipc_recv   os.wait, os.waitpid, os.wait3, os.wait4, os.waitid\n\nand rust's note grounds its choice in python's, quoting it: \"Reaping a child\ncollects its exit status - data received from another process.\"\n\nSo the answer to question 1 is `ipc_recv`, on shipped two-language precedent\nrather than on my preference, and this change makes the rest of the corpus\nagree with the two catalogues that already had it right. What the item found\nwas not an absent model of the return direction -- it was an INCONSISTENT one.\n\nTHE SHARPEST FORM OF THE INCONSISTENCY IS INSIDE ONE FILE. rust.yaml declares\n`Child::wait_with_output` as ipc_recv (mints untrusted_input) and\n`Command::output` as subprocess (mints nothing). The Rust stdlib implements\nthe second as spawn-then-`wait_with_output`; they return the identical\n`Output { stdout, stderr, status }`. Same bytes, same file, opposite answers.\n\nWHY I DID NOT SPLIT THE BOUNDARY, i.e. why INV-tutar is cited and not followed.\nINV-tutar split `env_read` because ONE BOUNDARY VALUE CARRIED TWO DIFFERENT\nFACTS -- a credential read and a host-description read -- and the auto-map read\nthe wrong one, which cost `host-secret-*` 48 of 85 adjudicated flows at 22.9%\nprecision. That is not this shape. A pipe from a child process IS\ninter-process communication; `ipc_recv` means what the row means. The item's\nworry is a different one: that `ipc_recv` collapses into `untrusted_input`\nalongside net_recv and db_read, so a claim cannot tell \"the network told me\"\nfrom \"a program I launched told me\". IT CANNOT, AT THE LABEL. It can at the\nFINDING: WI-vazal carries `source_boundary` on every flow precisely because\n\"the label is many-to-one\", and `verify-claims` prints the per-boundary\nbreakdown -- the live repro below reports `[origins: 3 ipc_recv]`. A new label\nwould buy claim-level selectivity at the cost of a new vocabulary value in\nevery consumer, and the fact is already recoverable. Reversible if a claim\nauthor ever needs to select on it.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS `simultaneous: true`, NOT A REPLACEMENT ROW, AND THIS IS NOT THE\nSHAPE INV-zumin FORBIDS. A content-returning launch is BOTH a launch (control\nleaves, the arguments are a subprocess sink, the callee stays opaque) AND a\nreceive (the return value is the child's). Nothing is undecidable and nothing\nneeds disambiguating -- both are true at once, which is exactly the\n`scala.sys.process.Process.apply` case the marker was built for. INV-zumin\nforbids emitting several boundaries where EXACTLY ONE is true and the call site\ncannot tell which (that is WI-lipis's bare-local wrapper); it does not forbid\nrecording two facts that are both true. Consequence: NO CORE CODE CHANGED. The\nsink derivation iterates rows, so the subprocess sink survives;\n`declares_opaque_crossing` asks over every row, so opacity survives;\n`compute_boundary_map` already emits one chain per simultaneously-true boundary\n(INV-zumin's own fix), so the launch chain survives.\n\nTHE POPULATION IS THREE THINGS, NOT ONE, AND ADR-0049 ALREADY SEPARATES THEM.\nClassifying all 116 subprocess symbols in the 14 shipped catalogues by ruling 1\n(\"does this call return a value whose content the far side chose?\"):\n\n  (A) RETURNS THE CHILD'S CONTENT -- a transfer. 18 symbols, 8 languages.\n      go Cmd.Output/CombinedOutput; python check_output/getoutput/\n      getstatusoutput; rust Command::output; erlang os:cmd; elixir\n      System.cmd/shell; haskell readProcess + 3; javascript execSync/\n      execFileSync/spawnSync; scala ProcessBuilder.lineStream/lazyLines.\n      THIS IS WHAT SHIPPED.\n  (B) RETURNS A HANDLE the child will fill later -- Popen, ProcessBuilder.start,\n      Runtime.exec, Command::spawn, createProcess, popen, NSTask.launch,\n      Port.open, open_port, child_process.spawn/exec. This is the `net_listen`\n      shape one channel over: a DEFERRED crossing, disclosed and never minted.\n      NOT DONE HERE and filed separately rather than folded in silently.\n  (C) RETURNS ONLY AN EXIT STATUS OR UNIT -- Run, Start, call, check_call,\n      system, rawSystem, callProcess, the exec*/spawn* families. Unchanged.\n\nTWO OMISSIONS, DISCLOSED RATHER THAN SILENT.\n  * `subprocess.run` is category A only when the caller passes\n    `capture_output=` or `stdout=PIPE`, and the catalogue matches on NAME, not\n    on keyword arguments. Rowing it would mint a source at every\n    `subprocess.run` in the corpus (54 in beads alone), most capturing nothing.\n    Left out; the row says so.\n  * The shipped precedent is BROADER than what I followed. rust's `Child::wait`\n    / `try_wait` and python's `os.wait*` are rowed ipc_recv on the strength of\n    an EXIT STATUS. I did not extend that to `Command::status`, `os.system` or\n    `Cmd.Run`: eight bits the program chose is not the child's message, and\n    minting there would make the rule \"the word subprocess appears\" rather than\n    ruling 1. That is a narrowing of the precedent, in the conservative\n    direction, and it is a disagreement with two shipped rows rather than an\n    oversight.\n\nLIVE REPRO, the item's own shape, through the production CLI on a fresh cache.\nA Go program whose whole body is\n    out, _ := exec.Command(\"git\", \"log\", \"--format=%s\").Output()\n    exec.Command(\"sh\", \"-c\", string(out)).Run()\nagainst `untrusted-input-no-subprocess`:\n\n  BEFORE  confirmed_with_caveats -- \"No unsanitized untrusted_input data\n          reaches subprocess zone\", with the caveat NAMING `os/exec.Cmd.Output`\n          as a door the analysis could not see through. The tool was looking\n          straight at the crossing and filing it as opacity.\n  AFTER   violated, 3 flows, [origins: 3 ipc_recv].\n\nFAIL-CLOSED CONTROL, because this change has a removal hidden inside an\naddition. `CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES` lists `ipc_recv` BEFORE `subprocess`, so the\nreceive row now wins `lookup_with_module` and the PRIMARY `io_boundary` slot on\na `Cmd.Output` edge reads `ipc_recv`. Had `compute_boundary_map` bucketed by\nthat single slot, every content-returning launch would have left the\n`subprocess` bucket and `must_not_exist: subprocess` would have started\nconfirming over live launches -- a false all-clear shipped as a side effect of\na recall fix. The control claim `{boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true}`\nreports 3 chains in BOTH arms, and a test asserts it over the real shipped\ngo.yaml with a pre-registered refutation arm (drop the marker, the subprocess\nchain does vanish) so the control is discriminating rather than vacuously true.\n\nTHREE WI-jupaf TESTS BROKE AND WERE REWRITTEN TO ASK THE DURABLE QUESTION.\nThey pinned `lookup_with_module(\"os.cmd\").boundary == \"subprocess\"` and\n`all boundaries == {\"subprocess\"}` -- a sole-declaration proxy for \"row order\ncannot decide the outcome\". `declares_opaque_crossing`'s own docstring already\nrecords that the single-slot answer is unreliable for a multi-boundary\nprimitive (\"opacity can lose that race\", measured on 2 scala primitives);\nerlang `os:cmd` just joined them. The assertions now test what WI-jupaf\nactually measured -- a `subprocess` chain reaches the boundary map for an\n`os:cmd` edge, the call still declares an opaque crossing, no env row is back,\nand any multi-boundary set must declare WHY -- which is strictly stronger than\nthe slot check it replaces.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE PRECISION ESTIMATE THE BAND RULING ASKS FOR -- THREE ARMS, 16\nREPOSITORIES (measurement 0006's cohort), EVERY ARM COLD-ASSERTED.\n\n  arm A  dev                          692 evidence rows\n  arm B  catalogue rows only          743 rows   sink_before_source 3.0%\n  arm C  rows + the one-call gate     732 rows   sink_before_source 1.9%   << SHIPS\n         (arm A background rate                                     0.6%)\n\nA -> C, which is what a user gets: +40 rows, -0. NO pre-existing finding\nanywhere in the cohort was deleted, and all 15 ddg-CONFIRMED flows survive the\ngate. 12 of the 16 repositories are unchanged. Two verdicts move\n`inconclusive -> violated` (gocryptfs untrusted-input-no-host-fs, plausible\nuntrusted-input-no-subprocess).\n\nOf the 40 rows shipped, by hypergumbo's OWN walk field:\n     5 (12.5%)  ddg CONFIRMED at `precise` -- a demonstrated value route\n    12 (30.0%)  cross_function\n    10 (25.0%)  sink_before_source   <-- STILL THERE, see below\n     6 (15.0%)  structural, no walk possible\n     4 (10.0%)  escaped\n     3 ( 7.5%)  source_not_tracked\n\nWHAT THE GATE DOES NOT FIX, stated rather than implied by the improved number.\nIt answers \"the source call and the sink call are ONE call\". It does not answer\n\"a DIFFERENT sink call textually precedes the source call in the same\nfunction\", which is the remaining 25%. gocryptfs's `callFidoCommand` is the\nclean example, read back against source: `io.WriteString(in, s)` writes the\nchild's STDIN and `cmd.Output()` reads its stdout four lines later, so\n\"subprocess output reaches a file write\" is false and that single row is the\nwhole of its verdict move. plausible's is the OTHER shape and the gate abstains\nBY DESIGN: `readFileSize` calls `execSync` twice (gzip, then brotli), so the\nedge records two call lines, the pair COULD be real, and removal without\nevidence is the expensive direction. It happens to be false here. Both are\nfiled rather than smoothed.\n\nTHE CEILING, MEASURED AFTER THE FACT AND DISCLOSED AS SUCH. I did not measure\nemission shape before building, which is the discipline INV-zihor followed and\nthis item did not. On beads the new rows classify 23 call sites against 217\nfunctions containing such a call -- 10.6%. The cause is WI-lipis's defect on\nthe RECEIVER rather than the argument: `var cmd *exec.Cmd` resolves and matches\n(`status.go:getGitActivity`), `cmd := exec.Command(...)` does not\n(`import.go:countLinesInGitHEAD`). So the item's payoff on idiomatic Go is\nbounded by a gap WI-lipis's second deliverable is about, and the two are the\nsame defect family seen from opposite ends of the call.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES FOR WI-lipis's DISCLOSED RECALL REDUCTION -- LESS THAN I\nEXPECTED, AND THE NUMBER IS THE POINT. WI-lipis removed 7 rows that each\nwrapped a buffer filled from a subprocess's stdout, and this item was filed to\nre-represent that crossing. Checked row by row against arm C: exactly ONE of\nthe seven re-appears (beads `status.go:getGitActivity`). The other six do not,\nfor the ceiling reason above -- their `*exec.Cmd` receiver is a bare local, so\nthe `Output()` call is not classified at all. The crossing is now MODELLED in\nthe catalogue and still INVISIBLE at six of those seven sites. That is a\nrecall statement about the analyzer, not about the row, and it is the direct\nargument for doing WI-lipis's dataflow half next."  # c3cc
